is a product from domesticated animals, obtained at a place and time where the blood can run into a container and be swiftly consumed or processed. In many cultures, the animal is slaughtered. In some cultures and religions, blood is a taboo food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-blood-curd"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_curd"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Mao xue wang","dish/mao-xue-wang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"],["Mala (seasoning)","ingredient/mala-seasoning"]]}]},"dish/mexican-juice-bar":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mexican-juice-bar","n":"Mexican juice bar","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Mexican cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A Frutería or Mexican juice bar is a juice bar that primarily serves Mexican desserts, beverages, antojitos and other popular Mexican snack foods. Mexican juice bars are popular establishments in many parts of Mexico and more recently in Mexican American communities in the South-Western United States."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mexican-juice-bar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_juice_bar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","ref":"site/mexicoinmykitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","url":"https://www.mexicoinmykitchen.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Pati Jinich","ref":"site/patijinich-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"patijinich.com","note":"Recipe archive from the Mexican-born chef and host of the PBS series Pati's Mexican Table.","url":"https://patijinich.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Mo